What does static mean in C#?

In C#, the static keyword is used to declare a member of a class that belongs to the class itself rather than to any instance of the class. When a member is marked as static, it can be accessed without creating an instance of the class. Static members are shared among all instances of the class and are initialized when the class is loaded into memory.

Static members can include:

1. Static fields: Variables that are shared among all instances of the class.
2. Static properties: Properties that are associated with the class rather than with instances.
3. Static methods: Methods that can be called on the class itself, without needing an instance.
4. Static events: Events that are associated with the class rather than with instances.
5. Static classes: Classes that cannot be instantiated and can only contain static members.

Static methods can only access static members and cannot access instance members directly. They are often used for utility functions or operations that don't require instance-specific data.

Example:

public class MathUtils
{
    public static int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
}

// Usage
int result = MathUtils.Add(2, 3);

In this example, the Add method is static, so it can be called on the MathUtils class without creating an instance.

